    LINUX ADMINISTRATION PRACTICALS - FEDORA COMMAND SHEET
    Based on: TY B.Sc. IT Semester V Linux Administration Practical Manual
    Environment: Fedora Workstation / Fedora Server (VirtualBox or VMware)

    IMPORTANT
    ---------
    1. Use a Fedora VM, not your main Windows installation.
    2. Take a VM snapshot before storage, firewall, networking, or user-management practicals.
    3. Commands marked with sudo need an administrator password.
    4. Replace USERNAME, SERVER_IP, SHARE_NAME, DISK, and paths with your actual values.
    5. Do not copy commands that format a disk or edit /etc/fstab unless you understand the target.
    6. Run commands one section at a time. Some practicals require a second Fedora VM or another Linux/NFS/SMB server.
    7. Fedora uses DNF. The `yum` command may work as a compatibility command, but `dnf` is the native package manager.
    8. Practical 1's RHEL installation is not a Fedora command practical. Use Fedora ISO to create the VM; the Linux desktop and directory commands below apply after installation.

    PREREQUISITES
    -------------
    - Fedora installed and booting successfully in VirtualBox/VMware.
    - Internet connection in the VM.
    - A user with sudo privileges.
    - Terminal access.
    - Basic commands: pwd, ls, cd, mkdir, touch, cp, mv, rm, cat.
    - Recommended VM: 2 CPU cores, 4 GB RAM, 25 GB virtual disk.
    - For storage practical: an extra EMPTY virtual disk is recommended. Never experiment on your real disk.
    - For network storage: a second VM or a reachable NFS/SMB server.
    - For SSH: two Linux systems/VMs, or one VM plus another computer.
    - For local repository: Fedora RPM packages and createrepo_c.
    - Keep a snapshot before changing system configuration.

    INITIAL FEDORA SETUP
    --------------------
    Check Fedora version:
        cat /etc/fedora-release
        uname -r

    Update package metadata and installed packages:
        sudo dnf check-update
        sudo dnf upgrade --refresh -y

    Install common practical tools:
        sudo dnf install -y vim nano wget curl git tar gzip bzip2 acl attr \
          lsof psmisc procps-ng util-linux policycoreutils-python-utils \
          openssh-clients openssh-server httpd firewalld tuned lvm2

    Enable useful services:
        sudo systemctl enable --now sshd
        sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld

    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 1 - LINUX INSTALLATION, GUI AND DIRECTORY STRUCTURE
    ============================================================

    A. VM / Fedora installation preparation
    - Download the Fedora Workstation ISO from the official Fedora website.
    - Create a new Linux VM, attach the ISO, and install Fedora.
    - Select a virtual disk. Do not select or format your computer's physical disk.
    - Create a normal user and allow administrator privileges.

    B. Display/login manager and desktop environment
        echo "$XDG_SESSION_TYPE"
        echo "$XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP"
        systemctl status display-manager
        systemctl get-default

    C. Linux directory structure
        ls /
        ls -ld /home /etc /var /usr /tmp /boot /dev /proc /sys

    Meaning:
        /       root of filesystem
        /home   users' home directories
        /etc    system configuration
        /var    changing data and logs
        /usr    installed applications and shared files
        /tmp    temporary files
        /boot   boot files
        /dev    device files
        /proc   process and kernel information
        /sys    device and kernel information

    D. Filesystem and disk overview
        lsblk -f
        df -hT
        findmnt

    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 2 - BASIC SYSTEM COMMANDS AND TEXT FILES
    ============================================================

    Create practice files:
        mkdir -p ~/linux-practical/p2
        cd ~/linux-practical/p2
        printf "Sujal IT 35000\nRahul HR 25000\nAmit IT 30000\nPriya Sales 28000\n" > employees.txt
        printf "error: disk full\ninfo: started\nerror: timeout\n" > log.txt

    A. Input/output redirection
        echo "First line" > output.txt
        echo "Second line" >> output.txt
        cat output.txt
        ls /wrong-path 2> errors.txt
        cat errors.txt
        cat employees.txt | grep IT

    B. grep and regular expressions
        grep "IT" employees.txt
        grep -i "sujal" employees.txt
        grep -n "IT" employees.txt
        grep -v "IT" employees.txt
        grep "^S" employees.txt
        grep "0$" employees.txt
        grep "[0-9]" employees.txt
        grep "error" log.txt

    C. Login and switch users
        whoami
        who
        id
        sudo useradd student
        sudo passwd student
        su - student
        whoami
        exit

    Note: If the user already exists, useradd will report that. Do not create duplicate users.
    To remove this test user later (only if you created it and no longer need it):
        sudo userdel -r student

    D. Text-file commands
        cat employees.txt
        cut -d ' ' -f 1 employees.txt
        less employees.txt
        head -n 2 employees.txt
        tail -n 2 employees.txt
        sort employees.txt
        wc employees.txt
        wc -l employees.txt
        grep "IT" employees.txt
        awk '{print $1, $2}' employees.txt
        awk '$3 > 30000 {print $1, $3}' employees.txt
        awk 'BEGIN {print "Employee Report"} {sum += $3} END {print "Total:", sum}' employees.txt

    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 3 - FILE MANAGEMENT, LINKS, ARCHIVES, PERMISSIONS
    ============================================================

    Create a safe working folder:
        mkdir -p ~/linux-practical/p3
        cd ~/linux-practical/p3
        echo "Original file" > original.txt
        cp original.txt copy.txt
        mv copy.txt renamed.txt
        mkdir folder1
        mv renamed.txt folder1/
        ls -lR

    A. Hard and soft links
        echo "Link demonstration" > original.txt
        ln original.txt hardlink.txt
        ln -s original.txt softlink.txt
        ls -li original.txt hardlink.txt softlink.txt
        cat hardlink.txt
        cat softlink.txt

    B. Archives and compression
        tar -cvf backup.tar original.txt hardlink.txt
        mkdir extracted
        tar -xvf backup.tar -C extracted
        gzip -k original.txt
        gunzip -k original.txt.gz
        bzip2 -k original.txt
        bunzip2 -k original.txt.bz2
        tar -czvf backup.tar.gz original.txt hardlink.txt
        tar -xzvf backup.tar.gz -C extracted
        tar -cjvf backup.tar.bz2 original.txt hardlink.txt
        tar -xjvf backup.tar.bz2 -C extracted

    Note: `-k` keeps the original file. `star` is not installed by default on every Fedora release.
    If your syllabus specifically requires star, check availability first:
        dnf info star
    If available:
        sudo dnf install -y star
    Do not assume it is available in every configured repository.

    C. Ownership and basic permissions
        ls -l original.txt
        chmod u+x original.txt
        chmod g+w original.txt
        chmod o-r original.txt
        chmod 755 original.txt
        ls -l original.txt
        sudo chown student original.txt
        sudo chgrp student original.txt
        ls -l original.txt

    D. SUID, SGID and Sticky Bit (safe examples)
        mkdir shared
        chmod 1777 shared
        ls -ld shared
    Expected ending: drwxrwxrwt

        sudo mkdir /tmp/team-demo
        sudo chgrp student /tmp/team-demo
        sudo chmod 2775 /tmp/team-demo
        ls -ld /tmp/team-demo
    Expected group permission contains s: drwxrwsr-x

    Check an existing SUID program:
        ls -l /usr/bin/passwd
    Look for s in the owner's execute position. Do not add SUID to arbitrary programs.

    E. ACL
        touch aclfile.txt
        sudo setfacl -m u:student:rw aclfile.txt
        getfacl aclfile.txt
    Remove the specific ACL entry:
        sudo setfacl -x u:student aclfile.txt
    Remove all extended ACL entries:
        sudo setfacl -b aclfile.txt

    F. Default permissions / umask
        umask
        umask -S
        touch umask-file.txt
        mkdir umask-dir
        ls -l umask-file.txt
        ls -ld umask-dir
    Temporary example for current terminal only:
        umask 027
        umask

    G. Extended attributes
        touch attribute-file.txt
        setfattr -n user.note -v "Linux practical" attribute-file.txt
        getfattr -d attribute-file.txt
        setfattr -x user.note attribute-file.txt

