    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 5 - SOFTWARE MANAGEMENT (DNF/YUM/RPM/LOCAL REPO)
    ============================================================

    Fedora uses DNF. On many Fedora versions, `yum` is a compatibility command.

    A. Package information and search
        dnf --version
        yum --version
        dnf search httpd
        dnf info httpd
        rpm -qa | head

    B. Install, update and remove a package
        sudo dnf install -y httpd
        httpd -v
        sudo dnf check-update
        sudo dnf upgrade -y
        sudo dnf remove -y httpd

    C. RPM commands
        rpm -qa | head
        rpm -q bash
        rpm -qi bash
        rpm -ql bash
        rpm -qf /usr/bin/bash

    To install a local RPM file (only if you have a compatible trusted RPM file):
        sudo dnf install ./package-name.rpm
    To query a downloaded RPM:
        rpm -qpi package-name.rpm
        rpm -qpl package-name.rpm

    D. Local filesystem repository (requires RPM files)
    Create a repository folder and place trusted Fedora-compatible RPM files in it:
        sudo mkdir -p /var/local/myrepo
        sudo cp /path/to/*.rpm /var/local/myrepo/
    Install repository creator:
        sudo dnf install -y createrepo_c
    Create repository metadata:
        sudo createrepo_c /var/local/myrepo

    Create repo configuration:
        sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/local-file.repo > /dev/null <<'EOF'
    [local-file]
    name=Local File Repository
    baseurl=file:///var/local/myrepo
    enabled=1
    gpgcheck=0
    EOF

    Refresh and inspect:
        sudo dnf clean all
        sudo dnf makecache
        dnf repolist

    Note: Replace /path/to/*.rpm with the real directory containing RPM files.
    For a secure production repository, use package signature checking (gpgcheck=1) and trusted signing keys.
    Do not download random RPM files from unknown websites.

