    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 7 - STORAGE, PARTITIONS AND FILESYSTEMS
    ============================================================

    WARNING: Disk partitioning and formatting can permanently destroy data.
    Use only an extra EMPTY virtual disk added to your VM. Confirm the device name carefully.

    A. Inspect disks, partitions and filesystems
        lsblk
        lsblk -f
        sudo fdisk -l
        df -hT
        findmnt
        blkid

    B. MBR/GPT information
        sudo parted -l
        sudo fdisk -l

    C. SAFE filesystem practice using a disk-image file (does not partition a real disk)
        mkdir -p ~/linux-practical/p7
        cd ~/linux-practical/p7
        truncate -s 512M filesystem-demo.img
        LOOPDEV=$(sudo losetup --find --show filesystem-demo.img)
        echo "$LOOPDEV"
    Create an ext4 filesystem on this disposable loop device:
        sudo mkfs.ext4 "$LOOPDEV"
        sudo mkdir -p /mnt/fs-demo
        sudo mount "$LOOPDEV" /mnt/fs-demo
        df -hT /mnt/fs-demo
        echo "Filesystem test" | sudo tee /mnt/fs-demo/test.txt
        cat /mnt/fs-demo/test.txt
        sudo umount /mnt/fs-demo
        sudo losetup -d "$LOOPDEV"

    D. Mount/unmount a filesystem
    Use the loop-image steps above. Never run mkfs on a device unless you have verified it is the disposable test device.

    E. UUID and labels
        lsblk -f
        sudo blkid
    For a real extra virtual disk only, configure `/etc/fstab` after verifying its UUID.
    Always test an fstab edit before reboot:
        sudo mount -a
    If `mount -a` reports an error, correct the entry before rebooting.
    Do not copy a made-up UUID into fstab.

    F. Swap overview
        swapon --show
        free -h
        cat /proc/swaps
    Creating swap partitions requires a dedicated empty disk and instructor supervision.

    G. Logical Volume Management (LVM) - isolated loop-file demonstration
    This uses a disposable file, not your real disk. Install LVM tools:
        sudo dnf install -y lvm2
        cd ~/linux-practical/p7
        truncate -s 1G lvm-demo.img
        LOOPDEV=$(sudo losetup --find --show lvm-demo.img)
        echo "$LOOPDEV"
    Create the PV, VG and LV on this disposable loop device:
        sudo pvcreate "$LOOPDEV"
        sudo vgcreate vg_demo "$LOOPDEV"
        sudo lvcreate -L 500M -n lv_demo vg_demo
        sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vg_demo/lv_demo
        sudo mkdir -p /mnt/lvm-demo
        sudo mount /dev/vg_demo/lv_demo /mnt/lvm-demo
        df -hT /mnt/lvm-demo
    Cleanup after practical:
        sudo umount /mnt/lvm-demo
        sudo lvremove -y /dev/vg_demo/lv_demo
        sudo vgremove -y vg_demo
        sudo pvremove -y "$LOOPDEV"
        sudo losetup -d "$LOOPDEV"
        rm -f lvm-demo.img

    H. Swap overview and safe swap-file practice
        swapon --show
        free -h
        cat /proc/swaps
    Create a temporary 512 MB swap file (only if your VM has enough free disk space):
        sudo fallocate -l 512M /swapfile-practical
        sudo chmod 600 /swapfile-practical
        sudo mkswap /swapfile-practical
        sudo swapon /swapfile-practical
        swapon --show
    Disable and remove it after the demonstration:
        sudo swapoff /swapfile-practical
        sudo rm -f /swapfile-practical

    I. vfat, ext4 and xfs
    Check filesystem tools:
        command -v mkfs.vfat
        command -v mkfs.ext4
        command -v mkfs.xfs
    Install missing tools if available:
        sudo dnf install -y dosfstools e2fsprogs xfsprogs
    Formatting commands (ONLY on a disposable loop device or confirmed empty test partition):
        sudo mkfs.vfat /dev/LOOP_DEVICE
        sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/LOOP_DEVICE
        sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/LOOP_DEVICE
    Replace /dev/LOOP_DEVICE with the verified test device. These commands erase the target.

