    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 9 - SSH AND KEY-BASED AUTHENTICATION
    ============================================================

    Requires a second Linux system/VM or a remote host.

    A. SSH service on Fedora server
        sudo dnf install -y openssh-server
        sudo systemctl enable --now sshd
        systemctl status sshd
        hostname -I

    B. Connect from client
        ssh USERNAME@SERVER_IP

    C. Generate an SSH key with passphrase
        ssh-keygen -t ed25519
    Press Enter for the default file location and enter a passphrase when prompted.

    D. Copy public key to remote system
        ssh-copy-id USERNAME@SERVER_IP
        ssh USERNAME@SERVER_IP

    E. Related utilities
        ssh -V
        scp file.txt USERNAME@SERVER_IP:~
        sftp USERNAME@SERVER_IP

    Never share your private key file (usually ~/.ssh/id_ed25519). Share only the .pub public key.

    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 10 - APACHE WEB SERVER AND VIRTUAL HOST
    ============================================================

    A. Install and start Apache
        sudo dnf install -y httpd
        sudo systemctl enable --now httpd
        systemctl status httpd
        httpd -v

    B. Create a simple webpage
        echo '<h1>Fedora Apache Practical</h1>' | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html
        curl http://localhost

    C. Apache configuration files
        sudo apachectl configtest
        ls /etc/httpd/
        ls /etc/httpd/conf.d/

    D. Virtual host
    Create a test document root:
        sudo mkdir -p /var/www/site1
        echo '<h1>Site One</h1>' | sudo tee /var/www/site1/index.html

    Create virtual-host configuration:
        sudo tee /etc/httpd/conf.d/site1.conf > /dev/null <<'EOF'
    <VirtualHost *:80>
        ServerName site1.local
        DocumentRoot /var/www/site1
        <Directory /var/www/site1>
            Require all granted
        </Directory>
    </VirtualHost>
    EOF

    Check and reload:
        sudo apachectl configtest
        sudo systemctl reload httpd

    Local test:
        curl -H 'Host: site1.local' http://127.0.0.1

    For access from another computer, configure firewall and DNS/hosts appropriately. Do not expose a test server to the public internet without approval.

    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 11 - FIREWALL
    ============================================================

    Fedora normally uses firewalld. Check status:
        sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld
        sudo firewall-cmd --state
        sudo firewall-cmd --get-active-zones
        sudo firewall-cmd --list-all

    A. Allow Apache HTTP service
        sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=http
        sudo firewall-cmd --runtime-to-permanent
        sudo firewall-cmd --list-services

    Remove HTTP rule if no longer required:
        sudo firewall-cmd --remove-service=http
        sudo firewall-cmd --runtime-to-permanent

    B. iptables compatibility
        iptables --version
        sudo iptables -L -n -v

    Note: Fedora uses firewalld/nftables by default. Do not flush iptables rules or disable the firewall on a connected machine. If the practical specifically requires iptables rule creation, do it only in an isolated lab VM with instructor guidance.

    ============================================================
    PRACTICAL 12 - NETWORK STORAGE (NFS / CIFS / AUTOMOUNT)
    ============================================================

    Requires a second machine/server that exports an NFS or SMB share.
    Do not expect the client commands to work until a server/share exists.

    A. NFS client tools
        sudo dnf install -y nfs-utils
        showmount -e SERVER_IP

    Mount an existing NFS export (replace values):
        sudo mkdir -p /mnt/nfs-demo
        sudo mount -t nfs SERVER_IP:/EXPORT_PATH /mnt/nfs-demo
        findmnt /mnt/nfs-demo
        sudo umount /mnt/nfs-demo

    B. CIFS/SMB client tools
        sudo dnf install -y cifs-utils
        sudo mkdir -p /mnt/cifs-demo

    Mount an existing SMB share (replace values; prompted for password):
        sudo mount -t cifs //SERVER_IP/SHARE_NAME /mnt/cifs-demo -o username=USERNAME
        findmnt /mnt/cifs-demo
        sudo umount /mnt/cifs-demo

    C. Persistent mount through fstab
    First obtain the exact server export/share and credentials from the administrator.
    Back up fstab:
        sudo cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.backup
    Do not paste a guessed fstab line. Use the correct server path, mount point, filesystem type and options.
    After a carefully prepared entry, test:
        sudo mount -a
        findmnt

    D. Automount
    Automount configuration depends on the NFS/SMB server and systemd mount/automount unit names.
    Check systemd automount documentation or follow the exact instructor-provided server path. Do not create an automount entry with placeholder values.

